From: Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:10:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] pro1x: pro1x.power=on brings the power profile's kernel
 settings

One switch on the command line (freebsd/cmdline) for the power profile
(freebsd/pm/README.md, stage 6): with pro1x.power=on, kern.smp.disabled=0
and hw.psci_idle.enable=1 go into the environment unless the command line
sets them itself, before the kernel config's environment (pro1x.env,
which has kern.smp.disabled=1) is added. Both have to be in the
environment at boot: the CPUs start at SI_SUB_SMP, and psci_idle chooses
OS-initiated mode (the clusters' states) only when enabled then. The rest
of the profile is user space's (rc.conf, rc.d), which reads the same
kenv.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/sys/arm64/arm64/machdep_boot.c	2026-10-30 20:01:05.460341086 +0100
+++ b/sys/arm64/arm64/machdep_boot.c	2026-10-30 20:01:05.470287938 +0100
@@ -167,6 +167,28 @@
 		cmdline_set_env(linux_command_line, CMDLINE_GUARD);
 #ifdef PRO1X_RAMCONS
 		/*
+		 * Pro1X: the power profile (freebsd/pm/README.md, stage 6).
+		 * pro1x.power=on on the command line brings its kernel
+		 * settings: all CPUs, their PSCI idle states with the
+		 * clusters' (OS-initiated mode is chosen at boot). Below
+		 * what the command line says itself, above the kernel
+		 * config's environment (pro1x.env: one CPU), which follows.
+		 */
+		{
+			static const char *const power_env[] = {
+				"kern.smp.disabled",	"0",
+				"hw.psci_idle.enable",	"1",
+			};
+			char *pw = kern_getenv("pro1x.power");
+
+			if (pw != NULL && strcmp(pw, "on") == 0)
+				for (u_int i = 0; i < nitems(power_env); i += 2)
+					if (!testenv(power_env[i]))
+						kern_setenv(power_env[i],
+						    power_env[i + 1]);
+			freeenv(pw);
+		}
+		/*
 		 * Pro1X bring-up: keep the kernel config's static environment
 		 * (config(8) "env") next to the command line's, which wins:
 		 * the first of two equal names is the one found, so only
